# Extra defaults for catapult-launched Geran-2.
#
# AIRSPEED ENVELOPE - the single most important fix for "launches but
# won't climb / sinks back to the ground". This is a HIGH-wing-loading
# airframe: 200 kg over a 4.6875 m2 wing = ~42 kg/m2, comparable to a
# light jet, NOT a slow trainer. With CLmax~1.0 (and that only at ~40
# deg alpha, unusable), the speed at which lift can actually equal
# weight at a flyable alpha is ~40 m/s; a comfortable cruise is ~45
# m/s (the real Shahed-136 class cruises ~180 km/h = ~50 m/s). The
# generic base file (Tools/autotest/default_params/plane-jsbsim.parm)
# is tuned for the Rascal trainer and sets AIRSPEED_CRUISE 22 /
# AIRSPEED_MIN 10 - i.e. it tells TECS to fly this aircraft at roughly
# HALF its stall speed. TECS then pitches up to bleed to that
# impossible target, the wing mushes, and it sinks. Ground-truth CSV
# (/tmp/geran2_debug.csv) confirmed the mechanism directly: released at
# ~29 m/s the aircraft rotated to alpha~15 deg but lift was only ~940 N
# vs 1961 N weight, so it sank 3m to the ground within 1.3s. Setting a
# realistic envelope for THIS airframe (below) is what lets it fly the
# mission. Loaded after plane-jsbsim.parm, so these override it.
AIRSPEED_MIN    32
AIRSPEED_CRUISE 45
AIRSPEED_MAX    60
# Cruise-ish trim throttle; THR_MAX stays 100 from the base file so the
# climb-out isn't throttle-limited.
TRIM_THROTTLE   60

# The catapult (see libraries/SITL/SIM_JSBSim.cpp) now releases the
# aircraft at ~40 m/s - already well above stall and above this rotate
# speed - so takeoff rotation to the climb schedule triggers
# immediately, which is exactly what a catapult launch wants (the
# aircraft is committed to flying the instant it leaves the rail). 30
# = past the ~27 m/s theoretical stall, comfortably below release.
TKOFF_ROTATE_SPD 30.0

# TECS climb/sink authority. With 38 kW and ~650 N thrust available
# against ~350-430 N cruise drag there is a solid excess-power margin,
# so allow a real climb rate to the 41m/100m mission altitudes.
TECS_CLMB_MAX   6.0
TECS_SINK_MIN   3.0

# BANK LIMIT + NAV SMOOTHING - the fix for "climbs fine, then spirals
# into the ground on the first turn". The base plane-jsbsim.parm allows
# ROLL_LIMIT_DEG 65, which for this low-aspect-ratio delta is an
# aerobatic bank: ground-truth CSV showed that as soon as L1 turned
# left toward WP2 the roll controller banked all the way to -71 deg,
# the vertical component of lift collapsed (cos71 = 0.33), the nose
# dropped to -15 deg and it spiralled in at 60 m/s while the elevator
# was still commanding nose-up (a classic over-banked spiral dive, not
# a pitch problem). Capping the bank to a sane cruise value keeps the
# turn coordinated and the vertical lift intact; a longer L1 period
# makes the heading capture toward each waypoint gentler so it doesn't
# slam to the bank limit and overshoot.
ROLL_LIMIT_DEG  35
NAVL1_PERIOD    20

# The generic plane-jsbsim.parm sets SERVO2_REVERSED=1, tuned for a
# different reference airframe. For Geran-2's own elevon/elevator
# convention this reverses the pitch control loop: confirmed directly
# by comparing live catapult launches with SERVO2_REVERSED=1 (elevator
# saturates fighting itself, pitch diverges/oscillates and never
# recovers) vs =0 (elevator moves smoothly and pitch converges back
# toward level after the initial post-launch transient).
SERVO2_REVERSED 0

# Bit 2 (value 4) = DISABLE_TOFF_ATTITUDE_CHK. auto_takeoff_check()
# (ArduPlane/takeoff.cpp) normally requires pitch/roll to stay within
# bounds for its whole confirmation window before it hands off to real
# TECS/attitude control - before that, the aircraft coasts on a fixed
# pre-launch elevator/aileron output. If anything (e.g. the catapult's
# own rotation) pushes attitude out of bounds during that window, the
# check keeps failing, launch never confirms, and the aircraft keeps
# coasting on that fixed pre-launch output while diverging further -
# a chicken-and-egg loop that (confirmed directly) no amount of extra
# pre-release hold time escapes, since it's gated by attitude, not
# elapsed time. A catapult provides a known-good launch the instant it
# fires, so this attitude gate is unnecessary here.
FLIGHT_OPTIONS 4

ARMING_CHECK 0

# TRIED AND REVERTED: RLL2SRV_RMAX/PTCH2SRV_RMAX_UP/_DN capped at
# progressively tighter values (60 -> 30 -> 8 deg/s) to bound the
# post-launch roll excursion at its source. This helped at first (peak
# bank fell from ~78deg to ~24deg) but stopped helping below ~24deg,
# and ground-truth CSV logging showed why: with RLL2SRV_RMAX=8, the
# roll controller was visibly trying to correct (aileron command
# swinging from -0.17 to +0.15, the right direction) but capped so
# hard it only ever reached ~0.05 rad of actual surface deflection -
# nowhere near enough authority to arrest a sustained ~20 deg/s roll
# rate that stayed essentially constant regardless of aileron command.
# The rate cap fixed the FALSE-estimate-driven full-deflection
# overreaction (that's what the DCM launch-acceleration fix in
# AP_AHRS_DCM.cpp actually addresses), but then handicapped the
# controller's ability to apply a big, legitimate correction once one
# was actually needed - the opposite problem. Removed; rely on the
# DCM fix alone to prevent the false overreaction, and let the roll
# controller use full authority for real corrections.